.TH DU 1 "2026" "spicule" "spicule User Commands"
.SH NAME
du \- estimate file space usage
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B du
.RB [ \-a ]
.RB [ \-s ]
.RB [ \-k ]
.RB [ \-r ]
.RI [ file .\|.\|.]
.SH DESCRIPTION
For each
.I file
operand,
.B du
writes the space allocated to it, and (by default) to every directory
contained within it, to standard output, one line per entry reported.
With no operand,
.B du
operates on the current directory.
.PP
Reported space is each entry's actual on-disk allocation, not its
logical size: a sparse file, or one rounded up to a filesystem
allocation unit, reports the larger, real allocated figure.
.PP
Directories are walked so that each directory's own reported total
already includes everything found beneath it (descendants are always
reported, or at least folded into their parent's total, before the
directory containing them is). A symbolic link is never followed \(em
only its own small allocation is counted \(em so a link target is
never double-counted through more than one path to it, and a symlink
cycle cannot cause an infinite walk.
.PP
Each output line is the size, in the selected block unit, followed by
a tab character and the corresponding pathname.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-a
Write a line for every file encountered, not just for directories (and
for a
.I file
operand that directly names a plain file, which is always reported
regardless of this option).
.TP
.B \-s
Write only the grand total for each
.I file
operand, instead of one line per directory (and, with
.BR \-a ,
per file). If both
.B \-a
and
.B \-s
are given together,
.BR \-s 's
suppression wins: only the grand total for each operand is written.
.TP
.B \-k
Report sizes in units of 1024 bytes instead of the default 512-byte
units.
.TP
.B \-r
Write a diagnostic to standard error for each entry that could not be
accessed or read, instead of silently skipping it with no message.
Either way, such an entry still counts toward a nonzero exit status;
.B \-r
only controls whether a message is printed, not whether the entry is
skipped.
.PP
A
.B \-\-
argument ends option parsing, so that a
.I file
operand beginning with
.B \-
can still be given unambiguously.
.SH OPERANDS
.TP
.I file
The pathname of a file or directory to measure. Defaults to the
current directory if no operand is given.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Every operand, and every entry found while walking it, was measured
successfully.
.TP
.B >0
At least one operand could not be opened, or at least one entry
beneath an operand could not be accessed or read while walking it
(see
.B \-r
above for whether that produces a diagnostic).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR df (1)
